[#:seon.task{:name "an-escaped-pass-closes-the-reply-without-its-fault"
             :kind :function
             :intent "When a turn pass escapes after `seon.fault/fault!` committed, turn-step's finally (agent.clj ~515-558) closes the reply and the caller gets `:seon.await/closed-error` with no fault, although the receipt is on the throw's ex-data (`:seon.fault/recorded`). The causing agent meets the fault only in its next, unrelated run. Offer the recorded receipt (or the fault value) on the reply before closing it. Done when a regression whose pass escapes after recording a fault receives that fault as data in the same reply."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/turn-step seon.cluster.agent/evaluate-source! seon.fault/carried-receipt}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/stability-sweep-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-wrapped-stop-interrupt-is-recorded-as-a-core-fault"
             :kind :function
             :intent "turn-step catches only a bare InterruptedException (agent.clj ~551); a stop that interrupts a Datahike call surfaces as superv.async ExceptionInfo(InterruptedException) and is recorded as a core fault with no function entity, which never resolves (477a97dc, 67029fe7, 2026-09-27 02:08/02:25Z). Recognise the stop's interrupt anywhere in the bounded cause chain (cyclic chains: seon.fault/carried-receipt-hangs-on-cyclic-cause-chain). The same faults lost their agent: fault! puts `:seon.agent/id` into `:seon.error/source` (fault.clj ~286-292) but not into the recording request, so `:seon.error.occurrence/agent` is nil (error.clj ~1492) and for-agent's occurrence clause (error.clj ~2066) never reaches the agent; pass agent-id into the recording request. Done when a stop interrupting a wrapped Datahike call records no fault, and a fault recorded under an agent carries that agent on its occurrence."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/turn-step seon.fault/fault!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/stability-sweep-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "interrupted-rewalks-the-cause-chain",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "`seon.cluster.agent/interrupted?` walks .getCause with its own seen-set loop, a second copy of `seon.error.refusal/cause-links`. Replace the loop with (some #(instance? InterruptedException %) (`seon.error.refusal/cause-links` failure)), EQUIVALENT on every chain (both stop at the first repeat)."}
 #:seon.task{:name "reply-size-prints-sources-to-measure-them",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "`seon.cluster.agent/submit-source-in-projection` still computes `:seon.turn/reply-size` by printing sources and counting that text. Carry the size from the authored request text without printing its parsed forms. Keep reply-size's consumer and units explicit; a regression must show that measuring the reply never prints or realizes its values.",
            :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/submit-source!},
            :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/plan-research/r-read-notes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "finished-agents-and-their-branches-accumulate"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Default holds 185 agents, each with a `:seon.agent/branch` (measured through JVM eval_clj on default: 185 `:seon.agent/id` entities, 185 branch values), and bin/seon start --head captured about 110 agent branch heads at a restart. Agents from finished lanes and probes are never archived and their branches never unlinked, so every restart captures and restores them. Related: my.agent/retire-branch-refuses-a-finished-agent-as-admission-closed (retire at run exit). Done when a finished agent's branch is unlinked and the agent archived at its run's exit, and the agent count on default after a probe batch returns to its prior value."
             :must-read #{my.agent/retire-branch! seon.cluster.registry/retire-branch!}}
#:seon.task{:name "two-reconciler-passes-launch-one-attempt"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Card R1 revised by hybrid-start-a-flow.md (2026-10-11), which supersedes the assemble! shape here and the CACHE-1/CACHE-2 cards. `seon.flow/db<->flow` (the Flow proc that plans and starts every agent graph) derives `seon.flow/plan` from `seon.task/frontier`, open attempts, ended counts and the roster, writes the attempt and the agent in ONE main transaction (`seon.task/attempt-tx` with `:seon.task.attempt/fork-point`, the commit id) and calls `seon.flow/start-flow!` (phase H7) with that row; a second pass writes nothing and starts nothing (idempotent by `seon.id/id [handle n]`, `seon.cluster.registry/fork!` created? false). Nothing else starts a graph: `seon.cluster.host/host!`, `start!` and the per-branch db<->flow graph are deleted. Proofs: this test (two passes, one attempt, one graph), seon.cluster.agent-test/a-held-leaf-is-never-launched, .../a-successor-forks-from-its-predecessors-end-commit; the lightness proof is H7's. Files: src/seon/flow.clj, src/seon/cluster/agent.clj (db<->flow section), src/seon/cluster/host.clj, test/seon/cluster/agent_test.clj."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/db<->flow seon.flow/start-graph! seon.cluster.registry/fork! seon.task/frontier seon.context/current-state seon.profile/measure}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-start-a-flow.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/r1-db-flow-design.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "each-intent-names-one-roster-state"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Card R2. Intents to Flow verbs: :paused launches without resume or pauses a running graph; :running resumes or re-hosts; :stopped pauses, joins, stops and releases the SCI context keeping agent, branch and attempt; :cancelled interrupts, ends :cancelled with a reason and deletes the branch; cluster :paused wins; `seon.flow/start-graph!` resumes only under the intent. A table over the five intents."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/start-flow! seon.cluster.agent/stop-flow! seon.flow/start-graph!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/runtime-follows-db.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "disarm-splits-into-stop-and-cancel"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Card D3 (with R2). design-probes P3: `clojure.core.async.flow/stop` then `seon.flow/join-graph!` without `interrupt-turn!` exits after the current transform (Flow reads control with priority between transforms; a transform is never preempted), and `flow/pause` then `flow/ping-proc` under the same bound acknowledges the pass's end. So `seon.cluster.agent/stop-flow!` splits: a :stopped intent is `stop-graph!` minus the interrupt-turn! line, a :cancelled intent keeps it; the bound `:seon.config.agent/turn-completion-backstop-ms` covers a whole pass (its dial already adds the provider or evaluation limit); a pass past the bound gets today's typed join timeout with the entry kept. Proof: R2's table row for :stopped (seon.cluster.agent-test/each-intent-names-one-roster-state)."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/stop-flow! seon.flow/join-graph!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/runtime-follows-db.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/design-probes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-fresh-agents-environment-costs-one-fork"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Phase H5 of hybrid-start-a-flow.md, option A by root's decision: a fresh agent's environment is TWO SCI forks, the branch's program copy (`seon.sci.eval/branch-ctx`: `seon.sci.eval/base-ctx`'s copy, or `seon.sci.eval/fork-cluster-ctx` of a source already holding the program) and the agent's fork (`seon.sci.eval/fork-for-turn`), with no `seon.sci.eval/acquire!` and no history reads for an agent that never ran. One fork (the base as the shared content-keyed source, re-acquisition building a new base instead of `seon.sci.eval/acquire!` in place) is held for the owner as a REPL-path design question. Proof: seon.cluster.agent-test/a-fresh-agents-environment-costs-two-forks (two forks, custody is the branch connection, the whole acquisition under 25 ms and 15 MB; measured 14.5 ms / 9.1 MB, 25.6 ms / 16.6 MB with four forks before). Files: src/seon/sci/eval.clj, src/seon/cluster/agent.clj, test/seon/cluster/agent_test.clj."
             :must-read #{seon.sci.eval/base-ctx seon.sci.eval/branch-ctx seon.sci.eval/fork-for-turn seon.cluster.agent/acquire-context! seon.profile/measure}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-start-a-flow.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-paused-intent-starts-a-pingable-graph-that-runs-nothing"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Phase H6 of hybrid-start-a-flow.md, the :paused row of card R2 (task seon.cluster.agent/each-intent-names-one-roster-state). `seon.flow/start-graph!` takes the task's `:seon.task/execution` and calls `clojure.core.async.flow/resume` only for :running; Flow starts paused. Proof: this test: a graph started under :paused answers :paused from `clojure.core.async.flow/ping` for every proc, a wake offered to its mailbox runs no turn, `clojure.core.async.flow/resume` then runs it; graph start under 15 ms and 5 MB with three procs through `seon.profile/measure` (10 ms once card D2 deletes the schedule proc). Files: src/seon/flow.clj, src/seon/cluster/agent.clj, test/seon/cluster/agent_test.clj."
             :must-read #{seon.flow/start-graph! seon.cluster.agent/graph-definition seon.cluster.agent/start-flow! seon.profile/measure}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-start-a-flow.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/runtime-follows-db.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-received-message-is-written-on-the-recipients-branch-and-rendered-in-its-context"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Phase P7 of hybrid-agent-world.md (Opus lane; beside P4 and P5). Messages are channels: the agent graph's mailbox proc (`seon.cluster.agent/mailbox-step`, today one payload-free wake in-port on a sliding-buffer 1) gains a second in-port :seon.agent/inbox, a bounded buffer carrying the message value (new dial :seon.config.agent/inbox-size, default 64, resources/seon/schemas/seon.config.agent.edn), registered in the roster beside the wake channel so `seon.cluster.agent/channels` answers it for `seon.cluster.message/send!`'s offer!. On an inbox message the :io transform writes the message on the agent's OWN connection with `seon.cluster.message/send-call` (identity :seon.message/id, so a repeat is a no-op) and then emits its :seon.agent/episode signal, so the next turn's `seon.context/current-state` renders the inbox from the agent's own branch as today. Nothing is written in transit, nothing relays through main, no other writer touches the branch; a crash between offer and write loses the message (channels are ephemeral), and the sender resends by the same id. Root's graph has the same mailbox, so messages to root are written on main by root's own proc. Proofs: this test (after one offer the recipient's branch holds the message by identity, main and the sender's branch do not, the episode fired once and current-state shows the message; offer -> write under 30 ms warm through seon.profile/measure); a-resent-message-with-the-same-id-writes-nothing (two offers, one datom set, two episodes); a-message-to-root-takes-the-same-path (root's mailbox writes it on main). Files: src/seon/cluster/agent.clj (mailbox-step, graph-definition, routing), resources/seon/schemas/seon.config.agent.edn, test/seon/cluster/agent_test.clj."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/mailbox-step seon.cluster.agent/graph-definition seon.cluster.agent/channels seon.cluster.message/send-call seon.context/current-state seon.profile/measure}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-agent-world.md"}}]
